
---
const title = "Common Money Mistakes Small Business Owners Make";
const description = "Discover seven common financial mistakes Kenyan small business owners make and practical ways to manage cash flow, expenses, customer payments, and profit.";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <title>{title} | Biashivo Business Insights</title>

    <style>
      :root {
        font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
        color: #24364b;
        background: #f7fafc;
      }

      * { box-sizing: border-box; }
      body { margin: 0; }
      a { color: #008e74; }

      .container {
        width: min(1120px, 92%);
        margin: auto;
      }

      header {
        background: #0c2033;
        padding: 24px 0;
      }

      .header-inner {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: space-between;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        gap: 20px;
      }

      .brand {
        color: white;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-size: 23px;
        font-weight: 800;
        letter-spacing: 1px;
      }

      .brand span { color: #00c9a7; }

      .back-link {
        color: #d3e1e9;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-size: 14px;
      }

      .article {
        width: min(800px, 90%);
        margin: 65px auto 85px;
        background: white;
        padding: clamp(24px, 5vw, 65px);
        border: 1px solid #e2e9ef;
        border-radius: 16px;
      }

      .category {
        color: #008e74;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        font-weight: bold;
        font-size: 13px;
        letter-spacing: 1.5px;
      }

      h1 {
        color: #0c2033;
        font-size: clamp(32px, 5vw, 48px);
        line-height: 1.18;
        margin: 18px 0 24px;
      }

      .intro {
        font-size: 19px;
        line-height: 1.85;
        color: #43566a;
        margin-bottom: 38px;
      }

      h2 {
        color: #0c2033;
        font-size: 27px;
        line-height: 1.35;
        margin: 42px 0 15px;
      }

      p, li {
        font-size: 17px;
        line-height: 1.9;
      }

      ul { padding-left: 24px; }
      li { margin-bottom: 10px; }

      .example {
        background: #eaf4f3;
        border-left: 4px solid #00a989;
        padding: 18px 24px;
        border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
        margin: 28px 0;
      }

      .example p { margin: 0; }

      .tip {
        background: #f7fafc;
        border: 1px solid #e2e9ef;
        border-radius: 10px;
        padding: 18px 24px;
        margin: 20px 0;
      }

      .tip p { margin: 0; }

      .closing {
        margin-top: 48px;
        padding-top: 24px;
        border-top: 1px solid #e2e9ef;
      }

      .button {
        display: inline-block;
        margin-top: 20px;
        background: #00a989;
        color: white;
        padding: 14px 22px;
        border-radius: 8px;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-weight: bold;
      }

      .button:hover { background: #008e74; }

      footer {
        background: #071726;
        color: #cbd5df;
        text-align: center;
        padding: 32px 16px;
        font-size: 14px;
      }

      @media (max-width: 600px) {
        .article { margin: 25px auto 50px; }
        h2 { font-size: 23px; }
      }
    </style>
  </head>

  <body>
    <header>
      <div class="container header-inner">
        <a class="brand" href="/">BIASHI<span>VO</span></a>
        <a class="back-link" href="/#articles">
          ← Back to Business Insights
        </a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main>
      <article class="article">
        <div class="category">Business Growth • Financial Management</div>

        <h1>Common Money Mistakes Small Business Owners Make</h1>

        <p class="intro">
          Running a small business in Kenya takes determination,
          creativity, and hard work. But even when customers are
          buying, small financial mistakes can quietly reduce
          profits and make business growth difficult.
          Here are seven common mistakes and practical ways
          to avoid them.
        </p>

        <h2>1. Mixing Personal and Business Money</h2>

        <p>
          Many small business owners use the same money for
          household needs and business operations.
          This can make it difficult to know how much
          the business is actually earning.
        </p>

        <div class="example">
          <p>
            <strong>Example:</strong> A shop owner receives
            KSh 15,000 in customer payments and uses KSh 5,000
            for household shopping without recording it.
            Later, the owner cannot explain why the business
            has less cash than expected.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tip">
          <p>
            <strong>How to avoid it:</strong> Keep separate records
            for business and personal transactions. Where possible,
            use a dedicated business payment account or wallet.
            Record any money taken for personal use as an owner
            withdrawal rather than an operating expense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>2. Confusing Sales with Profit</h2>

        <p>
          High sales do not automatically mean high profits.
          Every product or service has costs that must be
          considered before determining what the business earned.
        </p>

        <div class="example">
          <p>
            <strong>Example:</strong><br />
            Monthly sales: KSh 80,000<br />
            Cost of goods sold: KSh 48,000<br />
            Operating expenses: KSh 20,000<br />
            <strong>Estimated net profit: KSh 12,000</strong>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tip">
          <p>
            <strong>How to avoid it:</strong> Calculate gross profit
            and net profit regularly. Include direct product costs
            and relevant operating expenses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>3. Failing to Record Small Expenses</h2>

        <p>
          Small daily expenses can add up quickly.
          Transport, mobile data, packaging, delivery charges,
          and transaction fees may appear insignificant individually,
          but together they affect profitability.
        </p>

        <div class="example">
          <p>
            <strong>Example:</strong> Spending KSh 200 every
            business day on unrecorded expenses adds up to
            KSh 5,200 over 26 working days.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tip">
          <p>
            <strong>How to avoid it:</strong> Record expenses
            as they happen, even when the amount seems small.
            Review expense categories every week.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>4. Selling on Credit Without Tracking Payments</h2>

        <p>
          Offering customers time to pay can help maintain
          relationships, but it also creates risks when
          outstanding balances are not tracked.
        </p>

        <p>
          Some businesses rely on WhatsApp conversations,
          handwritten notes, or memory to remember
          who still owes money.
        </p>

        <div class="example">
          <p>
            <strong>Example:</strong> A customer orders catering
            services worth KSh 25,000 and pays KSh 10,000 upfront.
            The remaining KSh 15,000 should be recorded
            with an agreed payment date.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tip">
          <p>
            <strong>How to avoid it:</strong> Record the customer's
            name, total order value, payments received,
            outstanding balance, and payment due date.
            Follow up professionally when payment is due.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>5. Setting Prices Without Understanding Costs</h2>

        <p>
          Some entrepreneurs set prices by copying competitors
          without calculating their own costs.
          This can lead to sales that generate very little profit.
        </p>

        <p>
          A cake baker, for example, must consider ingredients,
          packaging, delivery, labour, and other relevant costs
          before deciding on a selling price.
        </p>

        <div class="tip">
          <p>
            <strong>How to avoid it:</strong> Calculate the cost
            of delivering each product or service, then set
            a price that supports a sustainable margin
            while remaining competitive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>6. Ignoring Cash Flow</h2>

        <p>
          Profit and cash flow are not the same thing.
          A business may have profitable orders but still
          struggle to pay suppliers if customers haven't paid yet.
        </p>

        <div class="example">
          <p>
            <strong>Example:</strong> A small business expects
            KSh 60,000 from customers next month, but needs
            KSh 25,000 this week to purchase stock.
            Without enough cash available today, the business
            may struggle to fulfill new orders.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tip">
          <p>
            <strong>How to avoid it:</strong> Track money coming in,
            upcoming expenses, unpaid customer balances,
            and the cash currently available.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>7. Making Decisions Without Reviewing Business Records</h2>

        <p>
          When business owners do not review their numbers,
          they may continue selling unprofitable products,
          overspending, or missing opportunities to grow.
        </p>

        <p>
          A weekly review can help answer important questions:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>How much did we sell this week?</li>
          <li>Which expenses increased?</li>
          <li>Which customers still owe us money?</li>
          <li>Which products or services bring the best margins?</li>
          <li>Do we have enough cash for upcoming expenses?</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="tip">
          <p>
            <strong>How to avoid it:</strong> Set aside time each
            week to review sales, expenses, outstanding payments,
            and business performance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>Simple Financial Habits That Help SMEs Grow</h2>

        <p>
          You don't need complicated accounting software
          to begin managing your business finances better.
          Start with these practical habits:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>Record every sale and payment received.</li>
          <li>Track all business expenses consistently.</li>
          <li>Keep personal withdrawals separate from expenses.</li>
          <li>Monitor unpaid customer balances.</li>
          <li>Calculate your actual profit regularly.</li>
          <li>Review cash flow before making major purchases.</li>
          <li>Use reliable records to guide business decisions.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How Biashivo Plans to Help Small Businesses</h2>

        <p>
          Biashivo is being developed to make business management
          simpler for Kenyan and African entrepreneurs.
        </p>

        <p>
          Our goal is to bring sales, expenses, customers,
          orders, and profit tracking together in one
          easy-to-understand platform.
        </p>

        <p>
          Instead of searching through notebooks, spreadsheets,
          and WhatsApp messages, business owners should be able
          to see important business information in one place.
        </p>

        <div class="closing">
          <h2>Know your numbers. Grow your business.</h2>

          <p>
            Avoiding these common money mistakes can help
            small business owners build stronger financial
            habits, protect their cash flow, and make
            more informed decisions.
          </p>

          <a class="button" href="/#articles">
            ← Explore More Business Insights
          </a>

